{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0f88862ac17851a60169bb2f85be0c81918cb60e3728c2eed0", "created": 1773875077, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Clinical Trial Indirect Costs & Patient Support System (Transportation, Childcare, Extra Appointments)\",\n  \"summary\": \"This community centers on how clinical trial participation can create additional non-medical burdens for patients, particularly through extra appointments that generate indirect costs such as transportation and childcare. The network links clinical trials to extra appointments, and then to indirect-cost categories (transportation, child care/childcare), while also highlighting how a patient’s support system—friends, family members, and peers—can mitigate these burdens through practical help (rides, meals) and participation support (discussing informed consent, note-taking, emotional support). Overall, the entities describe an access-and-adherence ecosystem where logistical and social supports interact with trial requirements to shape the patient experience.\",\n  \"findings\": [\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Extra appointments are the main operational driver of indirect costs in the community\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The core causal chain in the community is that clinical trial participation may require additional visits beyond standard care, and these extra appointments can produce downstream burdens for participants. This relationship is explicitly represented by the link between CLINICAL TRIAL and EXTRA APPOINTMENTS, which notes that added visits are a normal part of trial involvement and can create added time, travel, or related expenses [Data: Relationships (10496); Entities (4837)].\\n\\nThe same pathway is reinforced by the connection between INDIRECT COSTS and EXTRA APPOINTMENTS, which frames non-medical costs as arising because extra appointments are required [Data: Relationships (11254); Entities (5106, 4837)]. Together, these links indicate that “extra appointments” function as the primary mechanism through which indirect costs materialize in this community.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Transportation is a prominent indirect cost and also a practical support need\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Transportation is explicitly characterized as a potential indirect cost associated with clinical trial participation, particularly because extra appointments increase travel requirements and expenses [Data: Relationships (1048); Entities (740, 4837)]. This positions transportation as a key access barrier and logistical requirement within the trial context.\\n\\nAt the same time, transportation is also presented as something a support system can provide or arrange, indicating the community is not only about cost categories but also about practical mitigation through social support. The SUPPORT SYSTEM is directly linked to TRANSPORTATION as a key form of practical assistance during diagnosis and treatment, emphasizing reliable access to appointments and care logistics [Data: Relationships (1437); Entities (951, 740)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Childcare/child care is another highlighted indirect cost tied to extra trial visits\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Child care/childcare appears as a second major indirect-cost category that can arise when participation requires additional appointments beyond usual routines. The community explicitly connects CHILD CARE to CLINICAL TRIAL as an indirect cost arising due to extra appointments [Data: Relationships (1050); Entities (741)].\\n\\nThis is further grounded by the link between CHILDCARE and EXTRA APPOINTMENTS, which gives childcare as a concrete example of an indirect cost caused by extra visits [Data: Relationships (13614); Entities (4263, 4837)]. The presence of both CHILD CARE and CHILDCARE entities reflects the same practical burden (care arrangements/expenses) expressed in slightly different terms, both anchored to the extra-appointment requirement [Data: Entities (741, 4263); Relationships (1050, 13614)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The support system is a central coordinating concept for practical and emotional assistance\",\n      \"explanation\": \"SUPPORT SYSTEM functions as an organizing hub describing how patients receive help across multiple needs during diagnosis and treatment. It is directly associated with providing practical assistance—explicitly including transportation—indicating that support networks can reduce logistical barriers that might otherwise interfere with attending appointments [Data: Relationships (1437); Entities (951, 740)].\\n\\nThe community also frames “enlisting support” as a mechanism for building/using that network, tying practical tasks (transportation, meals) and emotional support into an actionable strategy rather than a passive resource. This is represented by the SUPPORT SYSTEM → ENLISTING SUPPORT relationship [Data: Relationships (5917); Entities (951, 3019)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Friends are positioned as trusted decision-support partners, including for informed consent\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Beyond logistics, the community highlights that friends can be involved in decision-making about clinical trial participation. Specifically, people are encouraged to discuss the informed consent form and decisions with friends or trusted others, embedding social consultation into the trial enrollment process [Data: Relationships (1035); Entities (733)].\\n\\nThis expands the community’s scope from material burdens (transportation/childcare) to informational and decisional support, where the patient’s social network can help interpret materials and think through participation choices [Data: Entities (733); Relationships (1035)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Family members are depicted as key helpers for appointments: note-taking, rides, and emotional support\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Family members are explicitly recommended to support patients during clinical interactions and logistics. The community links FAMILY MEMBER to NOTE-TAKING, indicating a concrete role during medical discussions/visits to help patients remember details and manage information [Data: Relationships (5356); Entities (2892)].\\n\\nFamily members are also identified as possible helpers who can provide transportation and emotional support, reinforcing that family support spans both practical access-to-care functions and coping support during diagnosis and treatment [Data: Relationships (5357, 5359); Entities (2892, 2894, 2895)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Peers broaden the support system beyond family and friends, reinforcing community-based assistance\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The network includes PEERS as non-family individuals (e.g., fellow patients or support-group peers) who can become part of a person’s broader support system during diagnosis and treatment. This is explicitly represented by the relationship identifying peers as potential members of the support system [Data: Relationships (1424); Entities (934, 951)].\\n\\nBy including peers, the community indicates support is not limited to existing personal relationships; it can include community or patient-network connections that contribute practical help and emotional encouragement, complementing family and friends [Data: Entities (934, 951); Relationships (1424)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Meals and other practical supports appear as complementary mitigation tools alongside transportation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"In addition to travel and childcare burdens, the community includes meals as a practical assistance category provided by a patient’s support system to reduce day-to-day burden during diagnosis and treatment. MEALS is linked to PROVIDING MEALS as the activity that delivers this support [Data: Relationships (5937); Entities (952)].\\n\\nThis complements transportation support in that both are routine, actionable interventions a support system can coordinate to help patients manage the added complexity of care and appointment schedules. The broader “enlisting support” concept explicitly includes arranging help for transportation and meals, tying these activities into a coherent support strategy [Data: Entities (3019, 952, 740); Relationships (5917)].\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"rating\": 3.6,\n  \"rating_explanation\": \"Impact severity is low-to-moderate because the community describes operational and social factors (indirect costs and support mechanisms) that can meaningfully affect clinical trial access and adherence but does not present indicators of harmful or illicit activity.\"\n}", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "stop"}}], "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1714, "prompt_tokens": 3771, "total_tokens": 5485, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": null, "reasoning_tokens": 132, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "text_tokens": null}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": null, "cached_tokens": 1664, "text_tokens": null, "image_tokens": null}}, "service_tier": null}}}